  "raw_text": "Earl Washington Jr. \u2014 false confession and DNA exoneration (Culpeper, Virginia)\n\nIn June 1982, nineteen-year-old Rebecca Lynn Williams was raped and murdered in her apartment in Culpeper, Virginia. Before she died she told police she had been attacked by a Black man acting alone. The case went unsolved for nearly a year.\n\nIn May 1983, Earl Washington Jr., a twenty-two-year-old Black farmhand with an intellectual disability placed around IQ 69, was arrested in a neighboring county on an unrelated charge. Over two days of questioning, investigators obtained statements from him confessing to five separate crimes, including the Williams murder. Four of the five confessions collapsed immediately as false and were dismissed. The murder confession went forward. Washington initially got the basic facts of the crime wrong \u2014 the victim's race, her height, the number of wounds, the location of the apartment \u2014 and was corrected and led toward accurate details across successive interrogation sessions. He was suggestible and eager to please the authority figures questioning him, agreeing with what was put to him.\n\nAt trial in 1984 the confession was effectively the entire case against him; the physical evidence did not implicate him. He was convicted of capital murder and sentenced to death. In 1985 he came within nine days of execution before attorneys obtained a stay.\n\nIn 1993, early DNA testing on crime-scene evidence indicated Washington was not the source, but the results were characterized ambiguously, and in January 1994 Governor L. Douglas Wilder commuted his sentence to life imprisonment rather than pardon him \u2014 vindication arriving as a half-measure that kept an innocent man in prison. In 2000, modern STR DNA testing definitively excluded Washington and matched a convicted rapist, Kenneth Tinsley. Governor James Gilmore granted an absolute pardon for the murder in October 2000, and Washington was released in February 2001, having served nearly eighteen years, more than nine of them on death row.\n\nIn 2006 a federal civil jury found that the state police investigator who took the confession, by then deceased, had fabricated confession evidence, and awarded Washington 2.25 million dollars against the investigator's estate. Tinsley pleaded guilty to the Williams murder in 2007.\n\nThe case became a canonical study in false confession: an intellectually disabled man supplied with the facts of a crime by his interrogators, a capital conviction resting entirely on a confession the physical evidence contradicted, and a system that needed seventeen years, two governors, and two generations of DNA technology to acknowledge what the evidence said the first time.",
